 which is *not* generally the perpendicular bisector of the segment CD. The statement is false in general and is not proved.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5 – Relation between P and O (perpendicular bisector of CD)\n> “By definition P is the circumcentre of ΔACD; thus P also lies on the perpendicular bisector of CD.  \n> Hence the line PO is exactly the perpendicular bisector of CD; in particular  \n> \\( PO\\perp CD\\) and \\( PO\\parallel AB.\\)”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe first part (P lies on the perpendicular bisector of CD) is correct because the circumcentre of ΔACD is equidistant from C and D. However, the conclusion that **the line PO coincides with the perpendicular bisector of CD** presupposes that O also lies on that bisector, which was never established (the position of O is still unknown). Moreover, the claim that \\(PO\\parallel AB\\) follows from the earlier (incorrect) claim that AB is perpendicular to CD. Since the perpendicular bisector of CD is not known to be parallel to AB, this step is unsupported.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** – depends on earlier false statements.\n\n---\n\n### Step 6 – Reflection of H across MN gives O\n> “Reflect H across the side MN; denote the image by H'.  \n> Because MN is the line of centres of the original circles, the reflection interchanges the two points P and O (both lie on the perpendicular bisector of CD and are symmetric with respect to MN).  \n> Consequently \\( H' = O.\\)”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nEven if P lies on the perpendicular bisector of CD, there is no justification that O also lies on that bisector **and** is symmetric to P with respect to MN. The symmetry argument is not proved; it assumes facts about O that have not been established. Hence the equality \\(H'=O\\) is unfounded.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 7 – Tangency of the line through O parallel to AP\n> “By (1) the direction of AP is orthogonal to the radius OE of ω … the line through O parallel to AP is tangent to ω at the point where it meets ω.”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe statement that AP is orthogonal to the radius OE is not proved. The tangent‑chord theorem relates the angle between a tangent and a chord to the angle subtended by the chord in the opposite arc; it does **not** give a perpendicularity between a chord (here AP) and a radius unless the chord is a diameter, which is not the case. No argument shows that the line through O parallel to AP meets ω at a point where the radius is perpendicular to the line. Therefore the claim of tangency is unjustified.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 8 – Final symmetry argument\n> “Since ℓ′ is tangent to ω and ℓ is the mirror image of ℓ′ with respect to MN, the line ℓ is also tangent to ω (a circle is symmetric with respect to any line through its centre).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nEven assuming the previous step were correct, the reflection of a tangent line about a line **through the centre** of the circle remains tangent. However, the line MN has **not** been shown to pass through the centre O of ω. The claim “a circle is symmetric with respect to any line through its centre” is true, but we do not know that MN passes through O. Hence the argument is incomplete.  \n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** – missing proof that MN contains O.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\nThe proof collapses at the very first step: the asserted properties of the spiral similarity are false, and every subsequent statement depends on those false properties. Consequently the solution does **not** constitute a valid proof of the problem statement."
