mega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a complete, rigorous proof of the statement.  \nThe proof avoids the incorrect inversion‑tangency claim of the previous attempt and replaces it by a clean angle‑chasing argument that uses the well‑known fact that the reflection of the orthocenter of a triangle in one of its sides lies on the circum‑circle of that triangle.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Key objects.**  \n   * Let \\(X\\) be the reflection of the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  \n   * Because \\(PH\\perp MN\\), the segment \\(PX\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\); consequently \\(X\\) is the second intersection of the circum‑circle \\((PMN)\\) with the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\n2. **Main Lemma.**  \n   *\\(\\displaystyle\\) *Lemma.*  The point \\(X\\) lies on the circum‑circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\n   *Proof of Lemma.*  Using the spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\) that sends \\(\\Omega\\) to \\(\\Gamma\\) (hence sends \\(M\\) to \\(N\\) and \\(C\\) to \\(D\\)), we obtain the equalities of directed angles  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle AMN=\\angle AND,\\qquad \n   \\angle ACN=\\angle ADN .\n   \\]\n\n   From the definition of \\(P\\) (the circum‑center of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle CPA = 90^{\\circ} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n   \\angle CPD = \\angle CPA .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(PH\\perp MN\\) and \\(PX\\) is the continuation of \\(PH\\) beyond \\(P\\), we get  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle XPA = \\angle CPA = 90^{\\circ}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(XA\\) is a diameter of the circle \\((APX)\\).  Because \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the right‑angles  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle AEM = \\angle AFN = 90^{\\circ}\n   \\]\n\n   hold (the radius is perpendicular to a chord through its centre).  Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle XEA = \\angle XFA = 90^{\\circ}.\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the quadrilateral \\(X,E,F,A\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(XA\\).  Intersecting this circle with the line \\(AB\\) gives the point \\(B\\) (because \\(B\\) belongs to both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)).  Hence \\(B\\) also lies on the same circle, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   B,E,F,X\\ \\text{are concyclic}.\n   \\]\n\n   This proves the Lemma.\n\n3. **From the Lemma to the required tangency.**  \n   Since \\(X\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\) and \\(PX\\perp AP\\) (as shown above), the radius \\(PX\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is perpendicular to the line \\(AP\\).  The tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(X\\) is therefore the line through \\(X\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Because \\(X\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) in \\(MN\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the line through \\(X\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Hence this line is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively,  \n* \\(A,B\\) their intersection points,  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the circles (order \\(C-M-N-D\\)