centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(PA\\) is a radius of that circle; therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (1) we have \\(PX\\) collinear with \\(PH\\); together with (6) this yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle XPA = 90^{\\circ}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the points \\(A,P,X\\) lie on a circle whose diameter is \\(XA\\); denote this circle by \\(\\gamma\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The quadrilateral \\(X,E,F,A\\) is cyclic\n\nFrom (5) we have \\(\\angle XEA = 90^{\\circ}\\) and \\(\\angle XFA = 90^{\\circ}\\) (both equal to \\(\\angle XPA\\) by (7)).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle XEA = \\angle XFA = 90^{\\circ},\n\\]\n\nwhich shows that the four points \\(X,E,F,A\\) are concyclic; the circle is precisely \\(\\gamma\\) (its diameter is \\(XA\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Inclusion of \\(B\\) – the Lemma\n\nThe point \\(B\\) belongs to both original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); applying the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) to the line \\(AB\\) we see that \\(AB\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal S\\).  Consequently \\(B\\) lies on the image of the circle \\(\\gamma\\) under \\(\\mathcal S\\).  But \\(\\gamma\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\) (its centre lies on \\(AP\\)), and \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the line \\(AP\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal S(\\gamma)=\\gamma\\).  Hence \\(B\\) also belongs to \\(\\gamma\\).\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nB,E,F,X\\ \\text{are concyclic},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(X\\) lies on the circum‑circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  This proves the Lemma announced in the sketch.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Tangency\n\nFrom (7) we have \\(PX\\perp AP\\).  In a circle, the radius drawn to a point of tangency is perpendicular to the tangent at that point.  Since \\(X\\in\\omega\\) and \\(PX\\) is a radius of \\(\\omega\\), the