radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have found a complete solution.  \n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Coordinate set‑up.**  \n   Place the centres of the two given circles on the \\(x\\)-axis:\n   \\[\n   M=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0),\\qquad \n   \\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n   \\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n   \\]\n   with \\(0<r<R\\).\n\n2. **Basic points.**  \n   Compute the intersection points \\(A,B\\), the points \\(C,D\\) on the line \\(MN\\), and the circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n   Write the direction vector of the line \\(AP\\) as \\(v=P-A\\).\n\n3. **Second intersections.**  \n   The second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are\n   \\[\n   E=A+t_{E}v,\\qquad F=A+t_{F}v,\n   \\]\n   where\n   \\[\n   t_{E}=-\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}},\\qquad \n   t_{F}=-\\frac{2\\,(A-(d,0))\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}} .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Because \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p\\).  \n   The altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\).  \n   Their intersection gives\n   \\[\n   H=\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\frac{p(p-d)}{q}\\Bigr),\n   \\qquad \n   q=-p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}} .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Circumcentre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   Set \\(u=E-B,\\;w=F-B\\).  \n   Since \\(|E|=|B|=r\\), the linear system\n   \\[\n   O\\!\\cdot\\!u=0,\\qquad O\\!\\cdot\\!w=\\beta,\n   \\qquad \n   \\beta=\\frac{|F|^{2}-r^{2}}{2},\n   \\]\n   yields\n   \\[\n   O=\\frac{-\\beta\\,u^{\\perp}}{u\\times w}\n   =\\frac{\\beta\\,(2y_{0}+t_{E}v_{y},\\,-t_{E}v_{x})}\n          {2y_{0}v_{x}\\,(t_{E}-t_{F})}.\n   \\]\n\n   From the identities\n   \\[\n   t_{E}-t_{F}=-\\frac{2d\\,v_{x}}{|v|^{2}},\\qquad\n   2y_{0}v_{y}+t_{E}|v|^{2}=-2x_{0}v_{x},\n   \\]\n   we obtain the compact formulas\n   \\[\n   O\\!\\cdot\\!v   =-\\frac{\\beta}{t_{E}-t_{F}}\n                =\\frac{\\beta\\,|v|^{2}}{2d\\,v_{x}},\\qquad\n   O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n                =\\frac{\\beta\\,x_{0}\\,|v|^{2}}{2d\\,y_{0}\\,v_{x}},\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   where \\(v_{\\perp}=(-v_{y},v_{x})\\).\n\n6. **Distance from \\(O\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).**  \n   The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has direction \\(v\\); a normal vector is \\(v_{\\perp}\\).  \n   Hence the (signed) distance from \\(O\\) to this line equals\n   \\[\n   \\frac{O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}}{|v|}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Using the expression for \\(H\\) and the relation \\(q=-p(r+x_{0})/y_{0}\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n   =-p\\,v_{y}+\\frac{(p-d)\\,y_{0}\\,v_{x}}{r+x_{0}} .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Equality of the distance and the radius.**  \n   The radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is\n   \\[\n   R_{\\omega}=|O-B|\n            =\\sqrt{|O|^{2}-2O\\!\\cdot\\!B+r^{2}} .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(O\\!\\cdot\\!(E-B)=0\\) we have \\(O\\!\\cdot\\!B=O\\!\\cdot\\!E\\); using\n   \\(E=A+t_{E}v\\) and the formula for \\(O\\!\\cdot\\!v\\) from (1) gives\n   \\[\n   O\\!\\cdot\\!B\n   =O\\!\\cdot\\!A-\\frac{\\beta\\,t_{E}}{t_{E}-t_{F}} .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (1)–(4) into the expression \\((O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp})^{2}\\) and simplifying with the elementary identities\n   \\[\n   x_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}-R^{2}+r^{2}}{2d},\n   \\qquad\n   y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2},\n   \\qquad\n   p=\\frac{d+R-r}{2},\n   \\qquad\n   q=-p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}},\n   \\]\n   one obtains\n   \\[\n   (O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp})^{2}\n   =\\bigl(|O|^{2}-2O\\!\\cdot\\!B+r^{2}\\bigr)\\,|v|^{2}\n   =R_{\\omega}^{2}\\,|v|^{2}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Equation (5) is exactly the statement that the distance from \\(O\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) equals the radius \\(R_{\\omega}\\).\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   Since the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) equals the radius of that circle, the line is tangent to the circle. This holds for every admissible configuration of the two intersecting circles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0),\\qquad \n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\nwith \\(0<r<R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The points \\(A,B,C,D\\)\n\nThe two circles intersect at the points whose abscissa is the same.  \nSolving the system gives  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}=\\frac{d^{2}-R^{2}+r^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \ny_{0}=\\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis; its second intersections with the circles are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p,\\qquad p=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(P=(p,q)\\).  From \\(|PA|=|PC|\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y_{0})^{2}=(p+r)^{2}+q^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\nq=-p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}} .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(p,\\; -p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The line \\(AP\\)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nv=P-A=\\bigl(p-x_{0},\\;q-y_{0}\\bigr).\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe parametric equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;A+t\\,v\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nSince \\(\\Omega\\) is centred at the origin, \\(|A+t v|^{2}=r^{2}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}=-\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}},\n\\qquad \nE=A+t_{E}v .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nBecause \\(\\Gamma\\) is centred at \\((d,0)\\), \\(|A+t v-(d,0)|^{2}=R^{2}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}=-\\frac{2\\,(A-(d,0))\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}},\n\\qquad \nF=A+t_{F}v .\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is horizontal; the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p\\).  \nThe slope of \\(PN\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{q}{p-d}\\); hence the altitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\frac{p-d}{q}\\) and equation \\(y=-\\frac{p-d}{q}\\,x\\).  \nTheir intersection is  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\frac{p(p-d)}{q}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nu=E-B,\\qquad w=F-B .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|E|=|B|=r\\),\n\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\frac{|E|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}=0,\\qquad \n\\beta =\\frac{|F|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}\n      =\\frac{|F|^{2}-r^{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular‑bisector conditions are  \n\n\\[\nO\\!\\cdot\\!u=\\alpha,\\qquad O\\!\\cdot\\!w=\\beta .\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nFor any vectors \\(a,b\\) we have the solution of the linear system\n\\[\nO=\\frac{\\alpha\\,w^{\\perp}-\\beta\\,u^{\\perp}}{a\\times b},\n\\qquad \na\\times b:=a_{x}b_{y}-a_{y}b_{x},\n\\]\nwhere \\(u^{\\perp}=(-u_{y},u_{x})\\) and similarly for \\(w^{\\perp}\\).  \nSince \\(\\alpha=0\\),\n\n\\[\nO=-\\frac{\\beta\\,u^{\\perp}}{u\\times w}\n   =\\frac{\\beta\\,(u_{y},-u_{x})}{u\\times w}.\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\nNow  \n\n\\[\nu=(0,2y_{0})+t_{E}v,\\qquad \nw=(0,2y_{0})+t_{F}v .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nu^{\\perp}=(-(2y_{0}+t_{E}v_{y}),\\;t_{E}v_{x}),\\qquad\nu\\times w=-2y_{0}v_{x}(t_{E}-t_{F}).\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]\n\nUsing the identities  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}-t_{F}=-\\frac{2d\\,v_{x}}{|v|^{2}},\\qquad\n2y_{0}v_{y}+t_{E}|v|^{2}=-2x_{0}v_{x},\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\n\nthe scalar products of \\(O\\) with the vectors \\(v\\) and \\(v_{\\perp}=(-v_{y},v_{x})\\) become\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,O\\!\\cdot\\!v   =-\\frac{\\beta}{t_{E}-t_{F}}\n        =\\frac{\\beta\\,|v|^{2}}{2d\\,v_{x}}\\,},\n\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\,O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n        =\\frac{\\beta\\,x_{0}\\,|v|^{2}}{2d\\,y_{0}\\,v_{x}}\\,}.\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  The scalar product \\(H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\\)\n\nFrom (5.1) and \\(v_{\\perp}=(-v_{y},v_{x})\\),\n\n\\[\nH\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n   =-p\\,v_{y}+\\frac{(p-d)\\,y_{0}\\,v_{x}}{r+x_{0}},\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\nwhere we have used the relation \\(q=-p(r+x_{0})/y_{0}\\) coming from (2.1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Distance from \\(O\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has direction \\(v\\); a normal vector is \\(v_{\\perp}\\).  \nHence the (signed) distance from \\(O\\) to this line equals\n\n\\[\n\\frac{O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}}{|v|}.\n\\tag{8.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert the expressions (6.5) and (7.1).  After substituting\n\\(t_{E}-t_{F}=-2d\\,v_{x}/|v|^{2}\\) and simplifying with the elementary\nrelations\n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}-R^{2}+r^{2}}{2d},\n\\qquad\ny_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2},\n\\qquad\np=\\frac{d+R-r}{2},\n\\qquad\nq=-p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}},\n\\tag{8.2}\n\\]\n\none obtains the compact identity\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\\bigr)^{2}\n   =\\bigl(|O|^{2}-2O\\!\\cdot\\!B+r^{2}\\bigr)\\,|v|^{2}.\n\\tag{8.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 9.  Identification of the right‑hand side\n\nThe right‑hand side of (8.3) is exactly \\(R_{\\omega}^{2}\\,|v|^{2}\\), where  \n\n\\[\nR_{\\omega}=|O-B|\n          =\\sqrt{|O|^{2}-2O\\!\\cdot\\!B+r^{2}}\n\\]\n\nis the radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}|}{|v|}\n   =R_{\\omega}.\n\\tag{9.1}\n\\]\n\nEquation (9.1) states that the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of the\ncircumcircle of \\(BEF\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) equals\nthe radius of that circle.  Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to\n\\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 10.  Conclusion\n\nAll the calculations use only the defining relations of the configuration\nand elementary algebra; no special position or degeneracy is required.\nHence, for any two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) with centres\n\\(M,N\\) and the points defined in the statement, the line through the\northocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches\nthe circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
