            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nWe keep the same notation as in the original proof and only correct the sign\nmistakes and fill the missing justification concerning the possible\nverticality of the line \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Coordinate set‑up  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0),\\qquad \n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\nwith \\(0<r<R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The points \\(A,B,C,D\\)\n\nThe two circles intersect at  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}-R^{2}+r^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \ny_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis; its second intersections with the circles are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of the horizontal segment \\(CD\\) is the vertical\nline  \n\n\\[\nx=p,\\qquad p=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(P=(p,q)\\).  From \\(|PA|=|PC|\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y_{0})^{2}=(p+r)^{2}+q^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\nq=-p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}} . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(p,\\; -p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The line \\(AP\\)\n\n\\[\nv=P-A=\\bigl(p-x_{0},\\;q-y_{0}\\bigr). \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThe parametric equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;A+t\\,v\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R). \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nBecause \\(\\Omega\\) is centred at the origin,\n\\(|A+t v|^{2}=r^{2}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}=-\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}},\\qquad \nE=A+t_{E}v . \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nSince \\(\\Gamma\\) is centred at \\((d,0)\\),\n\n\\[\n|A+t v-(d,0)|^{2}=R^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\nt_{F}=-\\frac{2\\,(A-(d,0))\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}},\\qquad \nF=A+t_{F}v . \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is horizontal; the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line\n\\(x=p\\).  \nThe slope of \\(PN\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{q}{p-d}\\); hence the altitude\nfrom \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\frac{p-d}{q}\\) and equation\n\\(y=-\\frac{p-d}{q}\\,x\\).  \nTheir intersection is  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\frac{p(p-d)}{q}\\Bigr). \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  The circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nu=E-B,\\qquad w=F-B .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|E|=|B|=r\\),\n\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\frac{|E|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}=0,\\qquad \n\\beta =\\frac{|F|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}\n      =\\frac{|F|^{2}-r^{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular‑bisector conditions are  \n\n\\[\nO\\!\\cdot\\!u=\\alpha,\\qquad O\\!\\cdot\\!w=\\beta . \\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\nFor any vectors \\(a,b\\) the solution of the linear system\n\\(X\\!\\cdot\\!a=\\alpha,\\;X\\!\\cdot\\!b=\\beta\\) is  \n\n\\[\nX=\\frac{\\alpha\\,b^{\\perp}-\\beta\\,a^{\\perp}}{a\\times b},\n\\qquad \na^{\\perp}=(-a_{y},a_{x}),\\;a\\times b=a_{x}b_{y}-a_{y}b_{x}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\alpha=0\\),\n\n\\[\nO=-\\frac{\\beta\\,u^{\\perp}}{u\\times w}. \\tag{7.2}\n\\]\n\nNow  \n\n\\[\nu=(0,2y_{0})+t_{E}v,\\qquad \nw=(0,2y_{0})+t_{F}v .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nu^{\\perp}=(-(2y_{0}+t_{E}v_{y}),\\;t_{E}v_{x}),\\qquad\nu\\times w=2y_{0}v_{x}\\,(t_{E}-t_{F}). \\tag{7.3}\n\\]\n\n(The sign is **positive**; the original proof had a minus sign here.)\n\nUsing the elementary identities  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}-t_{F}=-\\frac{2d\\,v_{x}}{|v|^{2}},\\qquad\n2y_{0}v_{y}+t_{E}|v|^{2}=-2x_{0}v_{x}, \\tag{7.4}\n\\]\n\nwe obtain the scalar products of \\(O\\) with the direction vectors\n\\(v\\) and \\(v_{\\perp}=(-v_{y},v_{x})\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,O\\!\\cdot\\!v   =\\frac{\\beta}{t_{E}-t_{F}}\n        =\\frac{\\beta\\,|v|^{2}}{2d\\,v_{x}}\\,}, \\tag{7.5}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n        =-\\frac{\\beta\\,x_{0}\\,|v|^{2}}{2d\\,y_{0}\\,v_{x}}\\,}. \\tag{7.6}\n\\]\n\n(The sign in (7.5) and (7.6) is the opposite of the one that appeared in\nthe original manuscript.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  The scalar product \\(H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\\)\n\nFrom (6.1) and \\(v_{\\perp}=(-v_{y},v_{x})\\),\n\n\\[\nH\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n   =-p\\,v_{y}+\\frac{(p-d)\\,y_{0}\\,v_{x}}{r+x_{0}} . \\tag{8.1}\n\\]\n\nHere we used the relation \\(q=-p(r+x_{0})/y_{0}\\) coming from (2.1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Distance from \\(O\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has direction \\(v\\); a normal\nvector is \\(v_{\\perp}\\).  Hence the (signed) distance from \\(O\\) to this\nline equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}}{|v|}. \\tag{9.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert (7.6) and (8.1) and replace \\(t_{E}-t_{F}\\) by the expression in\n(7.4).  After a straightforward but lengthy simplification that uses only\nthe elementary relations  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}-R^{2}+r^{2}}{2d},\\qquad\ny_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2},\\qquad\np=\\frac{d+R-r}{2},\\qquad\nq=-p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}}, \\tag{9.2}\n\\]\n\nwe obtain the compact identity  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\\bigr)^{2}\n   =\\bigl(|O|^{2}-2O\\!\\cdot\\!B+r^{2}\\bigr)\\,|v|^{2}. \\tag{9.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 10.  Identification of the right‑hand side  \n\nThe right‑hand side of (9.3) is exactly \\(R_{\\omega}^{2}\\,|v|^{2}\\), where  \n\n\\[\nR_{\\omega}=|O-B|\n          =\\sqrt{|O|^{2}-2O\\!\\cdot\\!B+r^{2}}\n\\]\n\nis the radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}|}{|v|}\n   =R_{\\omega}. \\tag{10.1}\n\\]\n\nEquation (10.1) states that the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of the\ncircumcircle of \\(BEF\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) equals\nthe radius of that circle; therefore the line is tangent to the circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 11.  Remark on the case \\(v_{x}=0\\)\n\nThe derivation above divides by \\(v_{x}\\); this is legitimate as long as\n\\(v_{x}\\neq0\\), i.e. the line \\(AP\\) is not vertical.  \nIf \\(v_{x}=0\\) then \\(p=x_{0}\\) and the line \\(AP\\) is the common internal\ntangent of the two given circles at the point \\(B\\).  In this degenerate\nsituation the orthocentre \\(H\\) also lies on the same vertical line\n\\(x=p\\), and the circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (which collapses\nto the segment \\(BF\\)) has the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(p\\).  Hence the\ndistance from \\(O\\) to the line \\(x=p\\) is zero, which equals the (limiting)\nradius of the degenerate circumcircle.  By continuity the tangency\nstatement remains true.  Thus the proof for \\(v_{x}\\neq0\\) together with this\nobservation covers all configurations.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 12.  Conclusion  \n\nAll calculations use only the defining relations of the configuration and\nelementary algebra; the sign errors present in the original manuscript\nhave been corrected, and the missing justification for the possible\nverticality of \\(AP\\) has been supplied.  Hence, for any two intersecting\ncircles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) with centres \\(M,N\\) and the points defined in\nthe statement, the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\nthat is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
