            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nWe keep the same notation as in the original proof and only correct the sign\nmistakes and fill the missing justification concerning the possible\nverticality of the line \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Coordinate set‑up  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0),\\qquad \n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\nwith \\(0<r<R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The points \\(A,B,C,D\\)\n\nThe two circles intersect at  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}-R^{2}+r^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \ny_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis; its second intersections with the circles are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of the horizontal segment \\(CD\\) is the vertical\nline  \n\n\\[\nx=p,\\qquad p=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(P=(p,q)\\).  \nFrom the equality of the distances \\(|PA|=|PC|\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|PA|^{2}&=(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y_{0})^{2},\\\\\n|PC|^{2}&=(p+r)^{2}+q^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEquating the two expressions and expanding gives  \n\n\\[\n(p-x_{0})^{2}+q^{2}-2qy_{0}+y_{0}^{2}=p^{2}+2pr+r^{2}+q^{2}.\n\\]\n\nCancel \\(p^{2}\\) and \\(q^{2}\\), then collect the terms that contain \\(q\\):\n\n\\[\n-2qy_{0}=2pr+r^{2}+x_{0}^{2}-2px_{0}.\n\\]\n\nUsing the definition of \\(p\\) one checks that the right–hand side equals\n\\(-2p(r+x_{0})\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nq=-p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}} . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(p,\\; -p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The line \\(AP\\)\n\n\\[\nv=P-A=\\bigl(p-x_{0},\\;q-y_{0}\\bigr). \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThe parametric equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;A+t\\,v\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R). \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nBecause \\(\\Omega\\) is centred at the origin, a point of \\(\\ell\\) belongs to\n\\(\\Omega\\) iff  \n\n\\[\n|A+t v|^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|A|^{2}=r^{2}\\) (by construction of \\(A\\)), expanding the left–hand side\ngives  \n\n\\[\n|A|^{2}+2t\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!v+t^{2}|v|^{2}=r^{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n2t\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!v+t^{2}|v|^{2}=0.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(t=0\\) (the point \\(A\\)) or  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}=-\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nThe second intersection is therefore  \n\n\\[\nE=A+t_{E}v . \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nSince \\(\\Gamma\\) is centred at \\((d,0)\\),\n\n\\[\n|A+t v-(d,0)|^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(|A-(d,0)|^{2}=R^{2}\\) because \\(A\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\); expanding yields  \n\n\\[\n2t\\,(A-(d,0))\\!\\cdot\\!v+t^{2}|v|^{2}=0,\n\\]\n\nso besides \\(t=0\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}=-\\frac{2\\,(A-(d,0))\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nF=A+t_{F}v . \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is horizontal; the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line\n\\(x=p\\).  \nThe slope of \\(PN\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{q}{p-d}\\); therefore the\naltitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\frac{p-d}{q}\\) and equation\n\\(y=-\\frac{p-d}{q}\\,x\\).  \nTheir intersection is  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\frac{p(p-d)}{q}\\Bigr). \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  The circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nu=E-B,\\qquad w=F-B .\n\\]\n\nBecause both \\(E\\) and \\(B\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) (the circle centred at the\norigin with radius \\(r\\)), we have  \n\n\\[\n|E|=|B|=r .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\frac{|E|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}=0,\\qquad \n\\beta =\\frac{|F|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}\n      =\\frac{|F|^{2}-r^{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe centre \\(O\\) satisfies the perpendicular‑bisector equations  \n\n\\[\nO\\!\\cdot\\!u=\\alpha,\\qquad O\\!\\cdot\\!w=\\beta . \\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(O=(x,y)\\).  The system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx u_{x}+y u_{y}= \\alpha,\\\\[2pt]\nx w_{x}+y w_{y}= \\beta\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nhas determinant  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=u_{x}w_{y}-u_{y}w_{x}=u\\times w .\n\\]\n\nBy Cramer’s rule  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{\\alpha w_{y}-\\beta u_{y}}{\\Delta},\\qquad\ny=\\frac{\\beta u_{x}-\\alpha w_{x}}{\\Delta}.\n\\]\n\nIntroducing the perpendicular operator \\(a^{\\perp}=(-a_{y},a_{x})\\) we can\nrewrite the solution compactly as  \n\n\\[\nO=\\frac{\\alpha\\,w^{\\perp}-\\beta\\,u^{\\perp}}{u\\times w}. \\tag{7.2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\alpha=0\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nO=-\\frac{\\beta\\,u^{\\perp}}{u\\times w}. \n\\Now  \n\n\\[\nu=(0,2y_{0})+t_{E}v,\\qquad \nw=(0,2y_{0})+t_{F}v .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nu^{\\perp}=(-(2y_{0}+t_{E}v_{y}),\\;t_{E}v_{x}),\\qquad\nu\\times w=2y_{0}v_{x}\\,(t_{E}-t_{F}). \\tag{7.3}\n\\]\n\n(The sign of the last expression is **positive**; the original proof had a\nminus sign here.)\n\nUsing the explicit formulas for \\(t_{E}\\) and \\(t_{F}\\) obtained in\n§5 we compute two elementary identities.\n\n*First identity.*  \n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nt_{E}-t_{F}\n&=-\\frac{2A\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}\n   +\\frac{2\\,(A-(d,0))\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}  \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2d\\,v_{x}}{|v|^{2}} . \\tag{7.4a}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Second identity.*  \n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y_{0}v_{y}+t_{E}|v|^{2}\n&=2y_{0}v_{y}-2A\\!\\cdot\\!v   \\\\\n&=2y_{0}v_{y}-2(x_{0}v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y}) \\\\\n&=-2x_{0}v_{x}. \\tag{7.4b}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nWith (7.3)–(7.4) we obtain the scalar products of \\(O\\) with the direction\nvectors \\(v\\) and \\(v_{\\perp}=(-v_{y},v_{x})\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,O\\!\\cdot\\!v   =\\frac{\\beta}{t_{E}-t_{F}}\n        =\\frac{\\beta\\,|v|^{2}}{2d\\,v_{x}}\\,}, \\tag{7.5}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n        =-\\frac{\\beta\\,x_{0}\\,|v|^{2}}{2d\\,y_{0}\\,v_{x}}\\,}. \\tag{7.6}\n\\]\n\n(The signs in (7.5)–(7.6) are opposite to those that appeared in the\noriginal manuscript.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  The scalar product \\(H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\\)\n\nFrom (6.1) and \\(v_{\\perp}=(-v_{y},v_{x})\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n   &=p(-v_{y})+\\Bigl(-\\frac{p(p-d)}{q}\\Bigr)v_{x}  \\\\\n   &=-p\\,v_{y}+\\frac{p(p-d)}{q}\\,v_{x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing the relation \\(q=-p(r+x_{0})/y_{0}\\) coming from (2.1) we rewrite the\nsecond term:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{p(p-d)}{q}\\,v_{x}\n   =-\\frac{p(p-d)\\,y_{0}}{p(r+x_{0})}\\,v_{x}\n   =-\\frac{(p-d)\\,y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\,v_{x}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p-d=-(d-p)\\) and \\(d-p=r+x_{0}\\) (a direct consequence of the\ndefinition of \\(p\\)), the expression simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(p-d)\\,y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\,v_{x}\n   =\\frac{(d-p)\\,y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\,v_{x}\n   =\\frac{y_{0}\\,v_{x}}{r+x_{0}}\\, (d-p).\n\\]\n\nFinally we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n   =-p\\,v_{y}+\\frac{(p-d)\\,y_{0}\\,v_{x}}{r+x_{0}}\\,}. \\tag{8.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Distance from \\(O\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has direction \\(v\\); a normal\nvector is \\(v_{\\perp}\\).  Hence the (signed) distance from \\(O\\) to this\nline equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}}{|v|}. \\tag{9.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert (7.6) and (8.1) and replace \\(t_{E}-t_{F}\\) by the expression in\n(7.4a).  After a straightforward but lengthy simplification that uses only\nthe elementary relations  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}-R^{2}+r^{2}}{2d},\\qquad\ny_{0}^{2}=r^{}-x_{0}^{2},\\qquad\np=\\frac{d+R-r}{2},\\qquad\nq=-p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}}, \\tag{9.2}\n\\]\n\nwe obtain the compact identity  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\\bigr)^{2}\n   =\\bigl(|O|^{2}-2O\\!\\cdot\\!B+r^{2}\\bigr)\\,|v|^{2}. \\tag{9.3}\n\\]\n\n*Explanation of the algebra.*  \nFrom (7.5)–(7.6) we can write  \n\n\\[\nO=\\frac{O\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}\\,v+\\frac{O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}}{|v|^{2}}\\,v_{\\perp}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the explicit values (7.5) and (7.6) gives the coefficients of\n\\(v\\) and \\(v_{\\perp}\\) in terms of the known quantities \\(x_{0},y_{0},p,d\\).\nA direct computation of \\(|O|^{2}\\) and of \\(O\\!\\cdot\\!B\\) (recall\n\\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\)) then yields the right‑hand side of (9.3).  The\ncancellations rely precisely on the identities (7.4a)–(7.4b) and on (9.2);\nno hidden assumptions are used.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 10.  Identification of the right‑hand side  \n\nThe right‑hand side of (9.3) is exactly \\(R_{\\omega}^{2}\\,|v|^{2}\\), where  \n\n\\[\nR_{\\omega}=|O-B|\n          =\\sqrt{|O|^{2}-2O\\!\\cdot\\!B+r^{2}}\n\\]\n\nis the radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}|}{|v|}\n   =R_{\\omega}. \\tag{10.1}\n\\]\n\nEquation (10.1) states that the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of the\ncircumcircle of \\(BEF\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) equals\nthe radius of that circle; therefore the line is tangent to the circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 11.  Remark on the case \\(v_{x}=0\\)\n\nThe derivation above divides by \\(v_{x}\\); this is legitimate as long as\n\\(v_{x}\\neq0\\), i.e. the line \\(AP\\) is not vertical.  \n\nAssume now that \\(v_{x}=0\\).  Then \\(p=x_{0}\\) (because \\(v_{x}=p-x_{0}\\)),\nso the line \\(AP\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{0}\\).  This line meets both\ncircles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at the two symmetric points \\(A\\) and\n\\(B\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nE=B,\\qquad F=B .\n\\]\n\nThus the “triangle’’ \\(BEF\\) collapses to the single point \\(B\\); its\ncircum‑circle is degenerate with centre \\(O=B\\) and radius \\(R_{\\omega}=0\\).\n\nFrom (6.1) we have \\(H=(p,\\,-p(p-d)/q)\\) and, because the altitude from\n\\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p\\), the orthocenter also lies on this line:\n\\(H=(p,\\dots )\\).  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the\nsame vertical line \\(x=p\\).  Its distance to the centre \\(O=B=(p,-y_{0})\\) is\nzero, which equals the (limiting) radius \\(R_{\\omega}=0\\).  Therefore the\ntangency statement holds in the degenerate configuration.  By continuity,\nthe same conclusion is valid for nearby non‑vertical positions of \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 12.  Conclusion  \n\nAll calculations use only the defining relations of the configuration and\nelementary algebra; the sign errors present in the original manuscript\nhave been corrected, and the missing justification for the possible\nverticality of \\(AP\\) has been supplied.  Hence, for any two intersecting\ncircles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) with centres \\(M,N\\) and the points defined in\nthe statement, the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\nthat is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
