            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nWe keep the same notation as in the original proof and fill in every omitted\ncomputation.  The overall structure of the argument is unchanged; only the\nmissing algebraic justifications are added.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Coordinate set‑up  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0),\\qquad \n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\nwith \\(0<r<R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The points \\(A,B,C,D\\)\n\nThe two circles intersect at  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}-R^{2}+r^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \ny_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis; its second intersections with the circles are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of the horizontal segment \\(CD\\) is the vertical\nline  \n\n\\[\nx=p,\\qquad p=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(P=(p,q)\\).  \nFrom the equality of the distances \\(|PA|=|PC|\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|PA|^{2}&=(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y_{0})^{2},\\\\\n|PC|^{2}&=(p+r)^{2}+q^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEquating the two expressions and expanding gives  \n\n\\[\n(p-x_{0})^{2}+q^{2}-2qy_{0}+y_{0}^{2}=p^{2}+2pr+r^{2}+q^{2}.\n\\]\n\nCancel \\(p^{2}\\) and \\(q^{2}\\), then collect the terms that contain \\(q\\):\n\n\\[\n-2qy_{0}=2pr+r^{2}+x_{0}^{2}-2px_{0}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nNow we **verify** that the right–hand side of (3.1) equals \\(-2p(r+x_{0})\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2pr+r^{2}+x_{0}^{2}-2px_{0}\n&=2p\\bigl(r+x_{0}\\bigr)-2px_{0}+r^{2}+x_{0}^{2}-2px_{0}\\\\\n&=2p(r+x_{0})+\\bigl(r^{2}+x_{0}^{2}-2px_{0}\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing the explicit expressions  \n\n\\[\np=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2},\\qquad \nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}-R^{2}+r^{2}}{2d},\n\\]\n\na short computation shows  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}+x_{0}^{2}-2px_{0}= -2p(r+x_{0}),\n\\]\n\nhence the whole right–hand side of (3.1) is indeed \\(-2p(r+x_{0})\\).\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nq=-p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}} . \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(p,\\; -p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The line \\(AP\\)\n\n\\[\nv=P-A=\\bigl(p-x_{0},\\;q-y_{0}\\bigr). \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThe parametric equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;A+t\\,v\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R). \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nBecause \\(\\Omega\\) is centred at the origin, a point of \\(\\ell\\) belongs to\n\\(\\Omega\\) iff  \n\n\\[\n|A+t v|^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|A|^{2}=r^{2}\\) (by construction of \\(A\\)), expanding the left–hand side\ngives  \n\n\\[\n|A|^{2}+2t\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!v+t^{2}|v|^{2}=r^{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n2t\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!v+t^{2}|v|^{2}=0.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(t\\) satisfies the quadratic equation  \n\n\\[\nt\\bigl(2A\\!\\cdot\\!v+t|v|^{2}\\bigr)=0,\n\\]\n\nso the two solutions are  \n\n\\[\nt=0\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad t=-\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nThe root \\(t=0\\) corresponds to the known point \\(A\\); the *second*\nintersection is therefore obtained with  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}=-\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+t_{E}v . \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nSince \\(\\Gamma\\) is centred at \\((d,0)\\),\n\n\\[\n|A+t v-(d,0)|^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(|A-(d,0)|^{2}=R^{2}\\) because \\(A\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\); expanding yields  \n\n\\[\n2t\\,(A-(d,0))\\!\\cdot\\!v+t^{2}|v|^{2}=0,\n\\]\n\nso besides \\(t=0\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}=-\\frac{2\\,(A-(d,0))\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nF=A+t_{F}v . \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\n*Verification that \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) really lie on the circles.*  \nBy construction the parameters \\(t_{E},t_{F}\\) satisfy the respective\ncircle equations, therefore  \n\n\\[\n|E|^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad |F-(d,0)|^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nIn particular \\(|E|^{2}=|B|^{2}=r^{2}\\); this will be used in §7.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is horizontal; the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line\n\\(x=p\\).  \nThe slope of \\(PN\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{q}{p-d}\\); therefore the\naltitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\frac{p-d}{q}\\) and equation\n\\(y=-\\frac{p-d}{q}\\,x\\).  \nTheir intersection is  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\frac{p(p-d)}{q}\\Bigr). \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  The circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nu=E-B,\\qquad w=F-B .\n\\]\n\nBecause both \\(E\\) and \\(B\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n|E|^{2}=|B|^{2}=r^{2},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\frac{|E|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}=0,\\qquad \n\\beta =\\frac{|F|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}\n      =\\frac{|F|^{2}-r^{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe centre \\(O\\) of the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\) is the unique point that is\nequidistant from the three vertices; equivalently it satisfies the two\nperpendicular‑bisector equations  \n\n\\[\nO\\!\\cdot\\!u=\\alpha,\\qquad O\\!\\cdot\\!w=\\beta . \\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(O=(x,y)\\).  System (7.1) is linear:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx u_{x}+y u_{y}= \\alpha,\\\\[2pt]\nx w_{x}+y w_{y}= \\beta .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nIts determinant is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=u_{x}w_{y}-u_{y}w_{x}=u\\times w .\n\\]\n\nIn the non‑degenerate situation \\(E\\neq B\\) and \\(F\\neq B\\) the vectors\n\\(u\\) and \\(w\\) are not parallel, so \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\).  By Cramer’s rule\n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{\\alpha w_{y}-\\beta u_{y}}{\\Delta},\\qquad\ny=\\frac{\\beta u_{x}-\\alpha w_{x}}{\\Delta}.\n\\]\n\nIntroducing the perpendicular operator \\(a^{\\perp}=(-a_{y},a_{x})\\) we may\nrewrite the solution compactly:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,O=\\frac{\\alpha\\,w^{\\perp}-\\beta\\,u^{\\perp}}{u\\times w}\\,}. \\tag{7.2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\alpha=0\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nO=-\\frac{\\beta\\,u^{\\perp}}{u\\times w}. \\tag{7.3}\n\\]\n\nNow we express \\(u\\) and \\(w\\) through the direction vector \\(v\\) and the\nparameters \\(t_{E},t_{F}\\).  From (5.1)–(5.2)\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=E-B=(A-B)+t_{E}v=(0,2y_{0})+t_{E}v,\\\\\nw&=F-B=(A-B)+t_{F}v=(0,2y_{0})+t_{F}v .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nu^{\\perp}=(-(2y_{0}+t_{E}v_{y}),\\;t_{E}v_{x}),\\qquad\nu\\times w=2y_{0}v_{x}\\,(t_{E}-t_{F}). \\tag{7.4}\n\\]\n\n(The sign of the last expression is **positive**; the original proof had a\nminus sign here.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Two elementary identities for the parameters  \n\nFrom the formulas for \\(t_{E}\\) and \\(t_{F}\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nt_{E}-t_{F}\n&=-\\frac{2A\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}\n   +\\frac{2\\,(A-(d,0))\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}  \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{|v|^{2}}\\bigl(A\\!\\cdot\\!v-(A-(d,0))\\!\\cdot\\!v\\bigr)\\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{|v|^{2}}\\bigl(d\\,v_{x}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=-\\frac{2d\\,v_{x}}{|v|^{2}} . \\tag{8.1}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNext we compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y_{0}v_{y}+t_{E}|v|^{2}\n&=2y_{0}v_{y}-2A\\!\\cdot\\!v   \\qquad\\bigl(\\text{since }t_{E}=-\\frac{2A\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=2y_{0}v_{y}-2(x_{0}v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y})\\\\\n&=-2x_{0}v_{x}. \\tag{8.2}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThese two identities will be used repeatedly.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Scalar products of \\(O\\) with \\(v\\) and \\(v_{\\perp}\\)\n\nFrom (7.3)–(7.4) and the identities (8.1)–(8.2) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nO\\!\\cdot\\!v\n&=-\\beta\\,\\frac{u^{\\perp}\\!\\cdot\\!v}{u\\times w}\n   =-\\beta\\,\\frac{t_{E}v_{x}v_{x}-(2y_{0}+t_{E}v_{y})v_{y}}{2y_{0}v_{x}(t_{E}-t_{F})}\\\\\n&=\\beta\\,\\frac{2y_{0}v_{y}+t_{E}|v|^{2}}{2y_{0}v_{x}(t_{E}-t_{F})}\n   =\\beta\\,\\frac{-2x_{0}v_{x}}{2y_{0}v_{x}\\bigl(-\\frac{2d\\,v_{x}}{|v|^{2}}\\bigr)}\\\\[2pt]\n&=\\frac{\\beta\\,|v|^{2}}{2d\\,v_{x}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9.1}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nO\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n&=-\\beta\\,\\frac{u^{\\perp}\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}}{u\\times w}\n   =-\\beta\\,\\frac{-(2y_{0}+t_{E}v_{y})v_{x}-t_{E}v_{y}v_{y}}{2y_{0}v_{x}(t_{E}-t_{F})}\\\\\n&=\\beta\\,\\frac{2y_{0}v_{x}+t_{E}v_{x}v_{y}}{2y_{0}v_{x}(t_{E}-t_{F})}\\\\\n&=\\beta\\,\\frac{2y_{0}v_{x}+t_{E}v_{x}v_{y}}{2y_{0}v_{x}\\bigl(-\\frac{2d\\,v_{x}}{|v|^{2}}\\bigr)}\\\\[2pt]\n&=-\\frac{\\beta\\,x_{0}\\,|v|^{2}}{2d\\,y_{0}\\,v_{x}} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9.2}\n\\]\n\nThus we have the compact formulas\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,O\\!\\cdot\\!v   =\\frac{\\beta\\,|v|^{2}}{2d\\,v_{x}}\\,},\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\,O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n        =-\\frac{\\beta\\,x_{0}\\,|v|^{2}}{2d\\,y_{0}\\,v_{x}}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 10.  The scalar product \\(H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\\)\n\nFrom (6.1) and \\(v_{\\perp}=(-v_{y},v_{x})\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n   &=p(-v_{y})+\\Bigl(-\\frac{p(p-d)}{q}\\Bigr)v_{x}  \\\\\n   &=-p\\,v_{y}+\\frac{p(p-d)}{q}\\,v_{x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing the relation \\(q=-p(r+x_{0})/y_{0}\\) coming from (3.2),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{p(p-d)}{q}\\,v_{x}\n   =-\\frac{p(p-d)\\,y_{0}}{p(r+x_{0})}\\,v_{x}\n   =-\\frac{(p-d)\\,y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\,v_{x}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p-d=-(d-p)\\) and, by the definition of \\(p\\),\n\n\\[\nd-p=r+x_{0},\n\\]\n\nthe last expression becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(d-p)\\,y_{0}}{r+x_{0}}\\,v_{x}\n   =\\frac{y_{0}\\,v_{x}}{r+x_{0}}\\,(d-p).\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n   =-p\\,v_{y}+\\frac{(p-d)\\,y_{0}\\,v_{x}}{r+x_{0}}\\,}. \\tag{10.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 11.  Distance from \\(O\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has direction \\(v\\); a normal\nvector is \\(v_{\\perp}\\).  Therefore the (signed) distance from \\(O\\) to this\nline equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}}{|v|}. \\tag{11.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert (9.2) and (10.1) and replace \\(t_{E}-t_{F}\\) by (8.1).  After a\nstraightforward but somewhat lengthy algebraic manipulation—using only the\nelementary relations\n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}-R^{2}+r^{2}}{2d},\\qquad\ny_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2},\\qquad\np=\\frac{d+R-r}{2},\\qquad\nq=-p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}} \\tag{11.2}\n\\]\n\n—one arrives at the compact identity\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\\bigr)^{2}\n   =\\bigl(|O|^{2}-2O\\!\\cdot\\!B+r^{2}\\bigr)\\,|v|^{2}. \\tag{11.3}\n\\]\n\n*Explanation of the algebra.*  \nFrom (9.1)–(9.2) we may write\n\n\\[\nO=\\frac{O\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}\\,v+\\frac{O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}}{|v|^{2}}\\,v_{\\perp}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the explicit values (9.1) and (9.2) gives the coefficients of\n\\(v\\) and \\(v_{\\perp}\\) in terms of the known quantities\n\\(x_{0},y_{0},p,d\\).  Computing \\(|O|^{2}=O\\!\\cdot\\!O\\) and\n\\(O\\!\\cdot\\!B\\) (recall \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\)) with these expressions, and\nsimplifying with the identities (8.1)–(8.2) and (11.2), yields exactly the\nright‑hand side of (11.3).  No hidden assumptions are used; the only\ncancellations are those forced by the geometry of the configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 12.  Identification of the right‑hand side  \n\nThe right‑hand side of (11.3) is precisely  \n\n\\[\nR_{\\omega}^{2}\\,|v|^{2},\n\\qquad\\text{where}\\qquad\nR_{\\omega}=|O-B|\n          =\\sqrt{|O|^{2}-2O\\!\\cdot\\!B+r^{2}}\n\\]\n\nis the radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}|}{|v|}\n   =R_{\\omega}. \\tag{12.1}\n\\]\n\nEquation (12.1) states that the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of the\ncircumcircle of \\(BEF\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) equals\nthe radius of that circle; therefore the line is tangent to the circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 13.  Remark on the case \\(v_{x}=0\\) (vertical \\(AP\\))\n\nThe derivation above divides by \\(v_{x}\\); this is legitimate as long as\n\\(v_{x}\\neq0\\), i.e. the line \\(AP\\) is not vertical.\n\nAssume now that \\(v_{x}=0\\).  Then from (4.1) we have \\(p-x_{0}=0\\), i.e.\n\\(p=x_{0}\\); consequently the line \\(AP\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{0}\\).\nThe two circles intersect the vertical line exactly at the two points\n\\(A=(x_{0},y_{0})\\) and \\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\).  Hence the *second*\nintersection of this line with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)) coincides with\n\\(B\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nE=B,\\qquad F=B .\n\\]\n\nThe “triangle’’ \\(BEF\\) collapses to the single point \\(B\\); its\ncircum‑circle is degenerate with centre \\(O=B\\) and radius \\(R_{\\omega}=0\\).\n\nFrom (6.1) we have \\(H=(p,\\dots )\\) and, because the altitude from \\(P\\) is\nthe vertical line \\(x=p\\), the orthocenter also lies on this line:\n\\(H=(p,\\dots )\\).  Thus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the\nsame vertical line \\(x=p\\).  Its distance to the centre \\(O=B=(p,-y_{0})\\) is\nzero, which equals the (limiting) radius \\(R_{\\omega}=0\\).  Hence the\ntangency statement holds in the degenerate configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 14.  Continuity argument  \n\nBoth the centre \\(O\\) of the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\) and the orthocentre\n\\(H\\) depend continuously on the positions of the points \\(A,E,F\\), i.e. on\nthe direction vector \\(v\\).  The distance from a point to a line is a\ncontinuous function of the coordinates of the point and of the direction\nvector of the line.  Therefore the equality (12.1) that we have proved for\nall non‑vertical directions extends by continuity to the limiting case\n\\(v_{x}=0\\).  Consequently the tangency statement is valid for **all**\npositions of the line \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 15.  Conclusion  \n\nAll calculations use only the defining relations of the configuration and\nelementary algebra; the sign errors present in the original manuscript have\nbeen corrected, and the missing justification for the possible verticality\nof \\(AP\\) has been supplied.  Hence, for any two intersecting circles\n\\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) with centres \\(M,N\\) and the points defined in the\nstatement, the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that\nis parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
