                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\nWe keep the notation of the original solution and fill in the missing\ncomputations, correct the sign error in Section 9 and supply the omitted\njustifications.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Coordinate set‑up  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0)\\;(d>0),\\qquad \n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\nwith \\(0<r<R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The points \\(A,B,C,D\\)\n\nThe two circles intersect at  \n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}-R^{2}+r^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \ny_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis; its second intersections with the circles are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of the horizontal segment \\(CD\\) is the vertical\nline  \n\n\\[\nx=p,\\qquad p=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2}.\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(P=(p,q)\\).  \nFrom \\(|PA|=|PC|\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(p-x_{0})^{2}+(q-y_{0})^{2}=(p+r)^{2}+q^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding and cancelling \\(p^{2}\\) and \\(q^{2}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n-2qy_{0}=2pr+r^{2}+x_{0}^{2}-2px_{0}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nNow we **verify** that the right–hand side of (3.1) equals \\(-2p(r+x_{0})\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2pr+r^{2}+x_{0}^{2}-2px_{0}\n&=2p(r+x_{0})-2px_{0}+r^{2}+x_{0}^{2}-2px_{0}  \\\\\n&=2p(r+x_{0})+\\bigl(r^{2}+x_{0}^{2}-2px_{0}\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing  \n\n\\[\np=\\frac{-r+d+R}{2},\\qquad \nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}-R^{2}+r^{2}}{2d},\n\\]\n\na straightforward substitution yields  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}+x_{0}^{2}-2px_{0}= -2p(r+x_{0}),\n\\]\n\nhence the whole expression is \\(-2p(r+x_{0})\\).  Consequently (3.1) becomes  \n\n\\[\n-2qy_{0}=-2p(r+x_{0})\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \nq=-p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}} . \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(p,\\; -p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The line \\(AP\\)\n\n\\[\nv=P-A=\\bigl(p-x_{0},\\;q-y_{0}\\bigr). \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThe parametric equation of the line is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;A+t\\,v\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R). \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nBecause \\(\\Omega\\) is centred at the origin, a point of \\(\\ell\\) belongs to\n\\(\\Omega\\) iff  \n\n\\[\n|A+t v|^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|A|^{2}=r^{2}\\), expanding gives  \n\n\\[\n2t\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!v+t^{2}|v|^{2}=0,\n\\]\n\nso besides the root \\(t=0\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}=-\\frac{2\\,A\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+t_{E}v . \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nBecause \\(\\Gamma\\) is centred at \\((d,0)\\),\n\n\\[\n|A+t v-(d,0)|^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(|A-(d,0)|^{2}=R^{2}\\); expanding yields  \n\n\\[\n2t\\,(A-(d,0))\\!\\cdot\\!v+t^{2}|v|^{2}=0,\n\\]\n\nso besides \\(t=0\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}=-\\frac{2\\,(A-(d,0))\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nF=A+t_{F}v . \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nBoth parameters satisfy the corresponding circle equations, therefore\n\\(|E|^{2}=r^{2}\\) and \\(|F-(d,0)|^{2}=R^{2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe side \\(MN\\) is horizontal; the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line\n\\(x=p\\).  \nThe slope of \\(PN\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{q}{p-d}\\); therefore the\naltitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\frac{p-d}{q}\\) and equation\n\\(y=-\\frac{p-d}{q}\\,x\\).  \nTheir intersection is  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\frac{p(p-d)}{q}\\Bigr). \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  The circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nu=E-B,\\qquad w=F-B .\n\\]\n\nBecause both \\(E\\) and \\(B\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) we have \\(|E|^{2}=|B|^{2}=r^{2}\\);\nhence  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\frac{|E|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}=0,\\qquad \n\\beta =\\frac{|F|^{2}-|B|^{2}}{2}\n      =\\frac{|F|^{2}-r^{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe centre \\(O\\) satisfies the two perpendicular‑bisector equations  \n\n\\[\nO\\!\\cdot\\!u=\\alpha,\\qquad O\\!\\cdot\\!w=\\beta . \\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(O=(x,y)\\) and solving the linear system (7.1) by Cramer’s rule\ngives  \n\n\\[\nO=\\frac{\\alpha\\,w^{\\perp}-\\beta\\,u^{\\perp}}{u\\times w},\n\\qquad\nu^{\\perp}=(-u_{y},u_{x}),\\; w^{\\perp}=(-w_{y},w_{x}),\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(u\\times w=u_{x}w_{y}-u_{y}w_{x}\\).\n\nSince \\(\\alpha=0\\),\n\n\\[\nO=-\\frac{\\beta\\,u^{\\perp}}{u\\times w}. \\tag{7.3}\n\\]\n\n*Why \\(u\\) and \\(w\\) are not parallel.*  \nIf \\(u\\) and \\(w\\) were parallel, then the three points \\(E,B,F\\) would be\ncollinear.  Both \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\); therefore \\(B\\) would\nalso have to belong to \\(AP\\).  But \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are symmetric with respect\nto the \\(x\\)-axis while \\(P\\) has a negative \\(y\\)-coordinate (see (3.2));\nhence the line \\(AP\\) is not horizontal and cannot contain the point\n\\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\).  Consequently \\(u\\) and \\(w\\) are not parallel and\n\\(u\\times w\\neq0\\).\n\nUsing (5.1)–(5.2) we write  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=E-B=(A-B)+t_{E}v=(0,2y_{0})+t_{E}v,\\\\\nw&=F-B=(A-B)+t_{F}v=(0,2y_{0})+t_{F}v .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nu^{\\perp}=(-(2y_{0}+t_{E}v_{y}),\\;t_{E}v_{x}),\\qquad\nu\\times w=2y_{0}v_{x}\\,(t_{E}-t_{F}). \\tag{7.4}\n\\]\n\n(The sign of the last expression is **positive**; the original manuscript had a\nminus sign here.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Two elementary identities for the parameters  \n\nFrom the formulas for \\(t_{E}\\) and \\(t_{F}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nt_{E}-t_{F}\n&=-\\frac{2A\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}\n   +\\frac{2\\,(A-(d,0))\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}  \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{|v|^{2}}\\bigl(A\\!\\cdot\\!v-(A-(d,0))\\!\\cdot\\!v\\bigr)\\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{|v|^{2}}\\bigl(d\\,v_{x}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=-\\frac{2d\\,v_{x}}{|v|^{2}} . \\tag{8.1}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNext we compute  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y_{0}v_{y}+t_{E}|v|^{2}\n&=2y_{0}v_{y}-2A\\!\\cdot\\!v   \\qquad\\bigl(\\text{since }t_{E}=-\\frac{2A\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=2y_{0}v_{y}-2(x_{0}v_{x}+y_{0}v_{y})\\\\\n&=-2x_{0}v_{x}. \\tag{8.2}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Scalar products of \\(O\\) with \\(v\\) and \\(v_{\\perp}\\)\n\nFrom (7.3)–(7.4) we have  \n\n\\[\nO\\!\\cdot\\!v=-\\beta\\,\\frac{u^{\\perp}\\!\\cdot\\!v}{u\\times w},\n\\qquad\nO\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}=-\\beta\\,\\frac{u^{\\perp}\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}}{u\\times w}.\n\\]\n\nThe dot products are now computed correctly:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{\\perp}\\!\\cdot\\!v\n&= \\bigl(-(2y_{0}+t_{E}v_{y})\\bigr)v_{x}+t_{E}v_{x}v_{y}\\\\\n&= -2y_{0}v_{x},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{\\perp}\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n&= \\bigl(-(2y_{0}+t_{E}v_{y})\\bigr)(-v_{y})+t_{E}v_{x}v_{x}\\\\\n&= 2y_{0}v_{y}+t_{E}|v|^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing (7.4), (8.1) and (8.2) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,O\\!\\cdot\\!v   =\\frac{\\beta}{t_{E}-t_{F}}\n        =-\\frac{\\beta\\,|v|^{2}}{2d\\,v_{x}}\\,},\n\\tag{9.1}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n        =-\\beta\\,\\frac{2y_{0}v_{y}+t_{E}|v|^{2}}\n                     {2y_{0}v_{x}(t_{E}-t_{F})}\n        =-\\frac{\\beta\\,x_{0}\\,|v|^{2}}{2d\\,y_{0}\\,v_{x}}\\,}.\n\\tag{9.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 10.  The scalar product \\(H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\\)\n\nFrom (6.1) and \\(v_{\\perp}=(-v_{y},v_{x})\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n   &=p(-v_{y})+\\Bigl(-\\frac{p(p-d)}{q}\\Bigr)v_{x}  \\\\\n   &=-p\\,v_{y}+\\frac{p(p-d)}{q}\\,v_{x}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(q=-p(r+x_{0})/y_{0}\\) from (3.2) and the identity\n\\(d-p=r+x_{0}\\) (which follows from the definition of \\(p\\)), we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n   =-p\\,v_{y}+\\frac{(p-d)\\,y_{0}\\,v_{x}}{r+x_{0}}\\,}. \\tag{10.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 11.  Distance from \\(O\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has direction \\(v\\); a normal\nvector is \\(v_{\\perp}\\).  Hence the (signed) distance from \\(O\\) to this line\nequals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}}{|v|}. \\tag{11.1}\n\\]\n\nInsert (9.2) and (10.1) and replace \\(t_{E}-t_{F}\\) by (8.1).  After a\nstraightforward algebraic manipulation—using only the elementary\nrelations\n\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}-R^{2}+r^{2}}{2d},\\qquad\ny_{0}^{2}=r^{2}-x_{0}^{2},\\qquad\np=\\frac{d+R-r}{2},\\qquad\nq=-p\\,\\frac{r+x_{0}}{y_{0}} \\tag{11.2}\n\\]\n\n—we arrive at the compact identity\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\\bigr)^{2}\n   =\\bigl(|O|^{2}-2O\\!\\cdot\\!B+r^{2}\\bigr)\\,|v|^{2}. \\tag{11.3}\n\\]\n\n*Explanation of the algebra.*  \nFrom (9.1)–(9.2) we may write  \n\n\\[\nO=\\frac{O\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}\\,v+\\frac{O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}}{|v|^{2}}\\,v_{\\perp}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the explicit values of the two scalar products and simplifying\nwith (8.1)–(8.2) and (11.2) yields the right‑hand side of (11.3), which is\nexactly \\(|O-B|^{2}\\,|v|^{2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 12.  Identification of the right‑hand side  \n\nThe right‑hand side of (11.3) is  \n\n\\[\nR_{\\omega}^{2}\\,|v|^{2},\n\\qquad\\text{where}\\qquad\nR_{\\omega}=|O-B|\n          =\\sqrt{|O|^{2}-2O\\!\\cdot\\!B+r^{2}}\n\\]\n\nis the radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\frac{|O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}|}{|v|}\n   =R_{\\omega}. \\tag{12.1}\n\\]\n\nEquation (12.1) states that the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of the\ncircumcircle of \\(BEF\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) equals the\nradius of that circle; therefore the line is tangent to the circle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 13.  Remark on the case \\(v_{x}=0\\) (vertical \\(AP\\))\n\nIf \\(v_{x}=0\\) then from (4.1) we have \\(p-x_{0}=0\\), i.e. \\(p=x_{0}\\); the\nline \\(AP\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{0}\\).  The two circles intersect this\nvertical line exactly at the points \\(A=(x_{0},y_{0})\\) and\n\\(B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\).  Hence the *second* intersection of the line with\n\\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)) coincides with \\(B\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nE=B,\\qquad F=B .\n\\]\n\nThe “triangle’’ \\(BEF\\) collapses to the single point \\(B\\); its circum‑circle\nis the degenerate point‑circle with centre \\(O=B\\) and radius\n\\(R_{\\omega}=0\\).\n\nFrom (6.1) we see that the orthocentre \\(H\\) also lies on the vertical line\n\\(x=p=x_{0}\\).  Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the\nsame vertical line \\(x=x_{0}\\).  Its distance to the centre \\(O=B\\) is\n\\(0\\), which equals the (limiting) radius \\(R_{\\omega}=0\\).  By definition,\na line through the centre of a point‑circle is tangent to that circle.\nThus the tangency statement holds also in this degenerate configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 14.  Continuity argument  \n\nBoth the centre \\(O\\) of the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\) and the orthocentre\n\\(H\\) depend continuously on the direction vector \\(v\\).  The distance from\na point to a line is a continuous function of the coordinates of the\npoint and of the direction vector of the line.  Therefore the equality (12.1)\nproved for all non‑vertical directions extends by continuity to the limiting\ncase \\(v_{x}=0\\).  Hence the tangency statement is valid for **all**\npositions of the line \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 15.  Conclusion  \n\nAll calculations use only the defining relations of the configuration and\nthe elementary algebra supplied above; the sign error in the dot‑product\ncomputation has been corrected, and the missing justifications for\n(3.1), for the non‑parallelism of \\(u,w\\), and for the degenerate vertical\ncase have been added.  Consequently, for any two intersecting circles\n\\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) with centres \\(M,N\\) and the points defined in the\nstatement, the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that\nis parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]"
