r all non‑vertical directions extends by continuity to the limiting case \\(v_{x}=0\\).\n\n*Justification Gap.*  The argument is plausible, but the author does not specify the precise functions or prove that no discontinuity occurs at the limit.  A rigorous proof would require showing that the formulas for \\(O\\) and \\(H\\) extend continuously as \\(v_{x}\\to0\\).\n\n---\n\n### 15. Conclusion  \n\n> The author summarises that all calculations are now justified and the required tangency statement holds.\n\n*Correct* provided the gaps identified above are filled.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution follows a clear coordinate‑geometry strategy and all the main algebraic identities are correct.  The only shortcomings are **omitted detailed calculations** in three places:\n\n1. Simplification of the RHS of (3.1) to \\(-2p(r+x_{0})\\);\n2. Derivation of the compact formula for the circum‑centre \\(O\\) from the linear system;\n3. The crucial algebraic reduction leading to identity (11.3).\n\nAdditionally, the treatment of the degenerate vertical case and the continuity argument are stated without formal justification.  These are **Justification Gaps**, not logical contradictions.  Consequently, the solution is **logically valid** once the gaps are supplied."
