ives \\(H=(p,\\,-\\frac{p(p-d)}{q})\\).\n\n**Verification:** In triangle \\(PMN\\), side \\(MN\\) is horizontal, so the altitude from \\(P\\) is indeed vertical \\(x=p\\). The slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\frac{q}{p-d}\\); the altitude from \\(M\\) is perpendicular, giving slope \\(-\\frac{p-d}{q}\\). Solving the two lines yields the stated coordinates. The computation is algebraically correct **if** the values of \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are the true coordinates of \\(P\\). Since \\(q\\) is wrong, the obtained point \\(H\\) is not the genuine orthocentre.  \n\n**Status:** Methodologically correct; numerically invalid due to the earlier critical error.\n\n---\n\n### 7. The circum\u2011centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\n> Set \\(u=E-B,\\; w=F-B\\).  \n> Because \\(|E|^{2}=|B|^{2}=r^{2}\\) we have \\(\\alpha=0\\), \\(\\beta=\\frac{|F|^{2}-r^{2}}{2}\\).  \n> Solve the linear system \\(O\\!\\cdot\\!u=\\alpha,\\; O\\!\\cdot\\!w=\\beta\\) to obtain  \n> \\[\n> O=-\\frac{\\beta\\,u^{\\perp}}{u\\times w}.\n> \\]\n\n**Verification:** The equations \\(O\\!\\cdot\\!u=\\alpha\\) and \\(O\\!\\cdot\\!w=\\beta\\) are the standard perpendicular\u2011bisector conditions for a circumcentre. The solution using Cramer\u2019s rule and the perpendicular vectors is correct. The claim that \\(u\\) and \\(w\\) are not parallel is justified later (the argument about \\(B\\) not lying on \\(AP\\) is sound). Hence this part of the construction is valid **provided** the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are correctly computed, which they are not because of the earlier error.  \n\n**Status:** Correct derivation; however, the numerical outcome depends on the erroneous \\(E,F\\).\n\n---\n\n### 8. Two elementary identities for the parameters  \n\n> Derive \\(t_E-t_F = -\\frac{2d\\,v_x}{|v|^{2}}\\) and \\(2y_{0}v_{y}+t_E|v|^{2} = -2x_{0}v_{x}\\).\n\n**Verification:** Starting from the expressions for \\(t_E\\) and \\(t_F\\) given in Section\u202f5, the algebraic manipulations are straightforward and correct. The identities hold for any vector \\(v\\).  \n\n**Status:** Correct.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Scalar products of \\(O\\) with \\(v\\) and \\(v_{\\perp}\\)\n\n> Using (7.3)\u2013(7.4) obtain  \n> \\[\n> O\\!\\cdot\\!v   =\\frac{\\beta}{t_{E}-t_{F}}\n>        =-\\frac{\\beta\\,|v|^{2}}{2d\\,v_{x}},\\tag{9.1}\n> \\]\n> \\[\n> O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n>        =-\\frac{\\beta\\,x_{0}\\,|v|^{2}}{2d\\,y_{0}\\,v_{x}}.\\tag{9.2}\n> \\]\n\n**Verification (Justification Gap):** The passage from the dot\u2011product expressions to the compact formulas (9.1) and (9.2) is asserted without showing the intermediate algebra. While the required substitutions (the values of \\(u^{\\perp}\\!\\cdot\\!v\\) and \\(u^{\\perp}\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\\) together with the identities from Section\u202f8) indeed lead to the stated results, the solution does not present these steps. Hence there is a **Justification Gap**. Moreover, because the earlier determination of \\(P\\) (and thus of \\(v\\)) is incorrect, the numerical validity of (9.1)\u2013(9.2) is compromised.\n\n**Status:** Justification Gap (and also affected by the critical error).\n\n---\n\n### 10. The scalar product \\(H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\\)\n\n> Using \\(q=-p(r+x_{0})/y_{0}\\) and the identity \\(d-p=r+x_{0}\\) obtain  \n> \\[\n> H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\n>   =-p\\,v_{y}+\\frac{(p-d)\\,y_{0}\\,v_{x}}{r+x_{0}}.\\tag{10.1}\n> \\]\n\n**Verification (Justification Gap):** The derivation uses the (incorrect) expression for \\(q\\) from (3.3). The algebraic manipulation itself is routine, but the solution does not display the intermediate steps, constituting a **Justification Gap**. Because \\(q\\) is wrong, the resulting formula for \\(H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\\) is also incorrect.\n\n**Status:** Justification Gap (and numerically invalid).\n\n---\n\n### 11. Distance from \\(O\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\n> The signed distance equals \\(\\frac{O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}}{|v|}\\).  \n> After substituting (9.2) and (10.1) and simplifying, one obtains  \n> \\[\n> (O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}-H\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp})^{2}\n>   =(|O|^{2}-2O\\!\\cdot\\!B+r^{2})\\,|v|^{2}. \\tag{11.3}\n> \\]\n\n**Verification (Justification Gap):** The claim that \u201cafter a straightforward algebraic manipulation\u201d the identity (11.3) follows is not substantiated. The manipulation involves many substitutions (including the erroneous expressions for \\(q\\) and for \\(v\\)) and cancellations; no intermediate steps are shown. Hence this is a **Justification Gap**. Moreover, because the underlying quantities are already incorrect, the equality cannot be trusted.\n\n**Status:** Justification Gap (and unreliable due to the critical error).\n\n---\n\n### 12. Identification of the right\u2011hand side  \n\n> Recognize \\(|O|^{2}-2O\\!\\cdot\\!B+r^{2}=|O-B|^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}\\), the squared radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n> Hence the distance from \\(O\\) to the line equals \\(R_{\\omega}\\), proving tangency.\n\n**Verification:** Assuming (11.3) were correct, the conclusion would indeed follow: the distance from a circle\u2019s centre to a line equals the radius iff the line is tangent. However, because (11.3) rests on the earlier flawed calculations, this step cannot be validated.\n\n**Status:** Dependent on previous errors; not independently justified.\n\n---\n\n### 13. Remark on the case \\(v_{x}=0\\) (vertical \\(AP\\))\n\n> If \\(v_{x}=0\\) then \\(E=B\\) and \\(F=B\\); the circumcircle degenerates to a point\u2011circle, and the vertical line through \\(H\\) (which coincides with \\(x=p\\)) is tangent to it.\n\n**Verification (Justification Gap):** The geometric reasoning that a line through the centre of a point\u2011circle is \u201ctangent\u201d is essentially a definition, but the solution does not explicitly state this. The argument that \\(E=B\\) and \\(F=B\\) when the line is vertical is correct.  \n\n**Status:** Minor Justification Gap.\n\n---\n\n### 14. Continuity argument  \n\n> Extends the equality (12.1) to the vertical case by continuity of rational functions.\n\n**Verification:** The idea that the formulas are rational functions of \\(v\\) and thus continuous where defined is sound. However, the solution does not verify that the denominators indeed stay non\u2011zero away from the vertical case, nor does it show the limit explicitly. This is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n**Status:** Justification Gap.\n\n---\n\n### 15. Conclusion  \n\n> Summarises that all calculations have been supplied and the tangency statement holds.\n\n**Verification:** The conclusion repeats the claim of correctness, but given the **Critical Error** identified in Section\u202f3, the conclusion is unsupported.\n\n**Status:** Invalid because of the earlier critical error.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe proof collapses at the very first algebraic simplification in Section\u202f3, where the right\u2011hand side of (3.2) is incorrectly transformed into \\(-2p(r+x_{0})\\). This mistake yields an erroneous coordinate for the circum\u2011centre \\(P\\), and every subsequent computation that depends on \\(P\\) (the direction vector \\(v\\), the points \\(E,F\\), the orthocentre \\(H\\), the circumcentre \\(O\\), and the final distance comparison) is built on a false premise. Consequently the argument does **not** establish the required tangency.\n\nIn addition to this fatal flaw, the solution contains several **Justification Gaps** where substantial algebraic steps are omitted (Sections\u202f9,\u202f11,\u202f12,\u202f13,\u202f14). While these gaps would need to be filled in a complete proof, they are rendered moot by the critical error already identified."

