ections that rely on \\(q\\) are therefore not validated.\n\n---\n\n### 4. The line \\(AP\\) – definition of \\(v\\)  \n\n> \\(v=P-A=(p-x_{0},\\,q-y_{0})\\).  \n> The parametric equation of the line is \\(A+t\\,v\\).\n\n**Verification:** This follows directly from the definition of a line through two points. No issue, but note that the coordinates of \\(v\\) contain the erroneous \\(q\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5. The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\n> Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\): solve \\(|A+t v|^{2}=r^{2}\\) → \\(t_{E}=-\\frac{2A\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}\\).  \n> Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\): solve \\(|A+t v-(d,0)|^{2}=R^{2}\\) → \\(t_{F}=-\\frac{2\\,(A-(d,0))\\!\\cdot\\!v}{|v|^{2}}\\).  \n\n**Verification (independent of \\(q\\)):** The derivation uses the fact that \\(A\\) lies on each circle, which is true. The quadratic equation reduces to the given linear factor because one root is \\(t=0\\). The formulas for \\(t_{E}\\) and \\(t_{F}\\) are correct.\n\n---\n\n### 6. The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\n> Since \\(MN\\) is horizontal, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p\\).  \n> The altitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\frac{p-d}{q}\\) and equation \\(y=-\\frac{p-d}{q}x\\).  \n> Their intersection is \\(H=(p,\\,-\\frac{p(p-d)}{q})\\).  \n\n**Verification:** The altitude from \\(P\\) is indeed the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\), i.e. the vertical line \\(x=p\\). The altitude from \\(M\\) must be perpendicular to \\(PN\\); the slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\frac{q}{p-d}\\), so the perpendicular slope is \\(-\\frac{p-d}{q}\\). Intersection of the two altitudes yields the stated coordinates. **However**, this step uses the (incorrect) value of \\(q\\); consequently the obtained coordinates for \\(H\\) are wrong. Because the error in \\(q\\) is critical, this step inherits the same critical error.\n\n---\n\n### 7. The circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\n> Set \\(u=E-B,\\; w=F-B\\).  \n> Because \\(|E|^{2}=|B|^{2}=r^{