nt of \\(q\\)):** The reasoning about the vertical line \\(AP\\) intersecting both circles again at the lower intersection point \\(B\\) is correct. The conclusion that the line through \\(H\\) (which also lies on the same vertical line) is tangent to the degenerate circle is valid.\n\n---\n\n### 13. Continuity argument  \n\n> They claim that because all expressions are rational functions of \\(v\\) and continuous for \\(v_{x}\\neq0\\), the equality extends to the limit \\(v_{x}\\to0\\).\n\n**Justification Gap:** The continuity of the *signed distance* and of the *radius* as functions of the configuration parameters is plausible, but a rigorous argument would need to verify that no hidden division\u2011by\u2011zero or sign changes occur in the limit. This step is therefore a **Justification Gap**.\n\n---\n\n### 14. Conclusion  \n\n> \u201cAll algebraic steps have been verified \u2026 therefore the line \u2026 is tangent \u2026\u201d\n\n**Overall Assessment:** Because the foundational formula for the \\(y\\)-coordinate of the circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) (Section\u202f3) is incorrect, every subsequent computation that depends on this value (the orthocentre \\(H\\), the scalar products involving \\(q\\), and the final distance comparison) is invalid. The proof therefore does **not** establish the required tangency. Moreover, several intermediate statements are presented without sufficient justification.\n\n--- \n\n**Summary of Issues**\n\n1. **Critical Error** \u2013 Wrong expression for \\(q\\) (Section\u202f3, formula\u202f(3.2)).  \n2. **Justification Gaps** \u2013 Perpendicular\u2011bisector equations for \\(O\\) (Section\u202f7).  \n3. **Justification Gaps** \u2013 Derivation of scalar products \\(O\\!\\cdot\\!v\\) and \\(O\\!\\cdot\\!v_{\\perp}\\) (Section\u202f9).  \n4. **Justification Gaps** \u2013 Distance formula (11.1) and the algebra leading to (11.10) (Section\u202f11).  \n5. **Justification Gaps** \u2013 Continuity argument extending the result to the degenerate case (Section\u202f13).  \n\nBecause of the critical error, the solution cannot be considered correct."

